# Precision-landing params for ArduPilot. PLND_TYPE=1 (MAVLink companion) as of
# 2026-09-15, Stage 2: our own src/mavlink_out.py now sends LANDING_TARGET instead of
# ArduPilot's built-in SIM_PLD simulator. Stage 1's SIM_PLD_* setup (verified working
# 2026-09-11, target acquisition/lateral correction/RTL all confirmed live) is kept
# commented below -- switch PLND_TYPE back to 4 and uncomment that block to fall back
# to it for a quick flight-control-only sanity check with no companion script running.
#
# Load with (from anywhere, on any machine -- no path to edit):
#   ./sim/run_sitl.sh
#
# That wrapper script resolves this file's path and ArduPilot's clone location
# automatically; see sim/README.md. If calling sim_vehicle.py directly instead, pass
# --add-param-file=<absolute path to this file> explicitly.
#
# Without this file, every one of these has to be typed by hand in the MAVProxy console
# after every SITL restart, and forgetting even one silently breaks precision landing with
# no obvious error pointing at which param is missing.

PLND_ENABLED     1     # enable-flag: without this, every other PLND_* param is hidden
PLND_TYPE        1     # 1 = MAVLink companion computer (src/mavlink_out.py). Was 4
                       # (ArduPilot's own SITL simulated target) through Stage 1.

# PLND_EST_TYPE=0 (RAW_SENSOR), not ArduPilot's own default of 1 (KALMAN_FILTER) --
# found 2026-09-24 after repeated "PrecLand: Target Found" -> "PrecLand: Init Failed"
# cycling in real Gazebo tests, never reaching "Init Complete". Root-caused by reading
# AC_PrecLand.cpp directly: EstimatorType::KALMAN_FILTER requires an update at least
# every EKF_INIT_SENSOR_MIN_UPDATE_MS (500ms) for a full EKF_INIT_TIME_MS (2000ms)
# window before target_acquired() ever returns true -- any single gap over 500ms during
# that window restarts the whole cycle. EstimatorType::RAW_SENSOR sets
# _target_acquired=true on the very first valid measurement, no warm-up at all.
# Confirmed as the deliberate, tested choice in a teammate's independently-built,
# formally-accepted reference project (github.com/format37/courierquad,
# sim/params/m0_sitl.param and m1_sitl.param both use EST_TYPE=0), not a guess.
PLND_EST_TYPE    0

# PLND_STRICT=1 (ArduPilot's own default -- "Retry Landing, Normal Strictness"), NOT
# the 0 ("Land Vertically, Not strict") this file had until 2026-09-24. That 0 was
# copied from a teammate's reference project (courierquad) without noticing WHY their
# board doesn't need retry: their board carries two co-planar tags (a large "outer" one
# for range, a small "inner" one for final descent) sized so something is always in
# frame. Our board has no such handoff -- our tag genuinely can leave the frame during
# descent, which is exactly what a live Gazebo test hit 2026-09-24: correction started,
# the tag left the FOV, and it landed off-target with no recovery attempt.
#
# ArduPilot already has a full built-in retry/climb mechanism for exactly this
# (AC_PrecLand_StateMachine.cpp: on a real target-lost timeout it climbs
# RETRY_OFFSET_ALT_M=1.5m and re-attempts, up to PLND_RET_MAX times) -- confirmed this
# works with our PLND_EST_TYPE=0 (RAW_SENSOR) setup too: target_acquired()'s staleness
# timeout (which feeds the retry trigger) is estimator-agnostic, not RAW_SENSOR-specific.
# PLND_ALT_MIN/PLND_ALT_MAX (a separate pair of retry-gating params) explicitly require
# a rangefinder we don't have -- left at their own defaults, effectively inert without
# one, but the actual retry trigger below (PLND_TIMEOUT) is purely time-based and needs
# no rangefinder.
PLND_STRICT      1
PLND_RET_MAX     4    # ArduPilot's own default -- max retry attempts before giving up
PLND_TIMEOUT     4    # ArduPilot's own default -- seconds to keep descending after
                      # target loss before actually retrying; revisit if 4s of blind
                      # descent is too long given how close to the ground our tag
                      # tends to disappear
PLND_RET_BEHAVE  0    # 0 = climb back to the last known target location (not just an
                      # approximate one) -- ArduPilot's own default

# CRITICAL, found 2026-09-24 by reading a real flight's dataflash log (00000026.BIN):
# PLND_STRICT=1 alone did NOT make retry engage -- "PrecLand: Retrying" never printed
# once in a ~24s continuous target-loss window, it just kept descending blind straight
# into "PrecLand: Failsafe Measures". Root cause: AC_PrecLand::check_if_sensor_in_range()
# treats the sensor as "out of range" whenever rangefinder_alt_valid is false UNLESS
# BOTH PLND_ALT_MIN and PLND_ALT_MAX are zero -- and ArduPilot's own compiled-in
# defaults for those (0.75, 8) are both nonzero. We have no rangefinder at all
# (RNGFND1_TYPE unset), so rangefinder_alt_valid is always false, which silently forces
# every single target loss straight to TARGET_OUT_OF_RANGE/TARGET_NEVER_SEEN --
# completely bypassing get_target_lost_actions()/retry_landing() regardless of
# PLND_STRICT. Setting both to 0 makes check_if_sensor_in_range() take its own
# "no limits specified, assume always in range" shortcut, which is the only way the
# retry state machine can ever actually run without a rangefinder.
PLND_ALT_MIN     0
PLND_ALT_MAX     0

# Descent-response tuning, 2026-09-18. A live run landed 0.70m off centre with the
# fused position drifting during descent. Root-caused via an offline replay of the
# actual flight's true (perfectly synchronised) attitude+position through our own
# detection/fusion code: the raw body-frame values we send ARE correct given vehicle
# tilt (e.g. 18 degrees roll at ~4.8m altitude legitimately produces ~1.5m of body-frame
# lateral offset even directly above the target -- MAV_FRAME_BODY_FRD is supposed to
# carry exactly this, with ArduPilot's own EKF rotating it into earth-frame using its
# more accurate internal attitude estimate). Not a bug in the camera/fusion geometry.
# NOTE 2026-09-24: this param only affects EstimatorType::KALMAN_FILTER -- since
# switching to PLND_EST_TYPE=0 (RAW_SENSOR, see above) this value is now INERT, left
# here only as a record of the earlier tuning attempt, not because it does anything.
PLND_ACC_P_NSE   1.25

# WPNAV_ACCEL, found 2026-09-24 by correlating a live flight's own dataflash log
# (00000028.BIN) against its vision log (logs/vision_20260924_174608.csv): the tag was
# only ever tracked in short 2-4s bursts before being lost again, repeatedly ("PrecLand:
# Target Found" -> "Target Lost" every 2.4-4.3s across the whole flight), and the fused
# body_x offset stayed frozen around -1.1m across MULTIPLE full retry cycles -- not
# oscillating, just never closing. Our camera is fixed (not gimbaled), so a lateral
# correction requires the vehicle to TILT to accelerate sideways, and that tilt swings
# the camera's already-narrow ~66deg FOV off-axis -- an aggressive correction can push
# the tag straight out of frame before it converges, which is exactly the repeating
# find/lose pattern in the log. mode_land.cpp confirms LAND's horizontal correction uses
# wp_nav->get_wp_acceleration_mss(), i.e. this param -- lowered from ArduPilot's own
# default (250 cm/s^2 = 2.5 m/s^2) to reduce tilt during correction, trading speed for
# keeping the tag in view long enough to actually converge. Not yet re-validated live.
WPNAV_ACCEL      100

# Camera-to-CG mounting offset. Left at zero because the synthetic camera has no
# physical offset to model (unlike CLAUDE.md section 8's PLND_CAM_POS_Z=-0.05 for the
# senior's real hardware) -- set explicitly here so it's a deliberate zero, not an
# unexamined default, and so it's obvious where to put a real value once real/Gazebo
# hardware geometry exists.
PLND_CAM_POS_X   0
PLND_CAM_POS_Y   0
PLND_CAM_POS_Z   0

# --- Stage 1 fallback: ArduPilot's own SIM_PLD simulator, no companion script needed ---
# Uncomment this block and set PLND_TYPE back to 4 above to use it again.
# SIM_PLD_ENABLE   1     # turn on the simulated target device itself
#
# The simulated beacon's location. Without these (all default to 0,0,0), ArduPilot
# correctly treats the target as "undefined" and never sees it, at any altitude --
# this was the first real bug found. Set to match sim/run_sitl.sh's default SITL
# start location (Amrita Vishwa Vidyapeetham, Bengaluru -- decided 2026-09-15,
# was CMAC before). If you override run_sitl.sh's --custom-location, update this
# to match, or the beacon and home will disagree and PrecLand will never engage.
# SIM_PLD_LAT      12.895444637941674
# SIM_PLD_LON      77.67580386901876
# SIM_PLD_HEIGHT   0        # metres above the SITL origin -- ground level at home
#
# Second real bug: AC_PrecLand::construct_pos_meas_using_rangefinder() requires either
# a real rangefinder (we have none -- RNGFND1_TYPE unset) OR the SITL backend's simulated
# "distance to target" to be nonzero. That distance is only ever nonzero when this option
# bit is set. Without it, "PrecLand: Target Found" can never print, regardless of beacon
# position or flight altitude -- the target's XY position can never be computed at all.
# SIM_PLD_OPTIONS  1        # bit 0 = ENABLE_TARGET_DISTANCE
# --- end Stage 1 fallback block ---

AUTO_OPTIONS     2     # bit 1 = AllowTakeOffWithoutRaisingThrottle. Without this, AUTO
                       # mode's mission-takeoff requires the pilot to physically raise the
                       # throttle stick as a safety confirmation and disarms immediately if
                       # it doesn't see that raise (ArduCopter/mode_auto.cpp comment: "note
                       # that this can defeat the disarm check on takeoff" -- exactly our
                       # SITL case, no real stick, rc 3 pinned low). GUIDED's `takeoff`
                       # command is unaffected -- this only matters for mission-driven AUTO
                       # takeoffs, found 2026-09-11 tracing ArduCopter/mode_auto.cpp directly.

# Left at SITL defaults, recorded here for reference (not required to be set explicitly):
#   SIM_PLD_ALT_LMT  15   -- max altitude (m) the simulated target is "visible" from
#   SIM_PLD_DIST_LMT 10   -- max lateral distance (m) from beacon it's visible from
#   PLND_EST_TYPE    1    -- Kalman filter estimator; needs ~2s of continuous good
#                            detections after "Target Found" before "Init Complete"
